<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=portland-cement">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portland cement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ordinary Portland cement, OPC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Portland cement is a hydraulic cement made by heating limestone and clay minerals in a kiln to form clinker, then grinding it with a little gypsum; it is the most common cement and the basic ingredient of concrete and mortar. Joseph Aspdin patented it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1824 and named it after Portland stone, quarried on the Isle of Portland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>. It grew out of the natural cements made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from the middle of the 18th century, John Smeaton’s experiments of 1756 and Louis Vicat’s artificial hydraulic lime. His son William Aspdin improved it in the 1840s at Rotherhithe, and the rotary kiln, patented in 1885, allowed continuous manufacture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portland-cement.jpg" alt="Portland cement" width="330" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KVDP · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portland_Cement_Bags.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Portland cement developed from natural cements made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from the middle of the 18th century. In 1756 John Smeaton experimented with combinations of limestones and additives, including trass and pozzolanas, for the lighthouse now known as Smeaton's Tower. James Parker patented Roman cement in 1796; it became popular but was largely replaced by Portland cement in the 1850s. Louis Vicat's artificial hydraulic lime is considered the principal forerunner of Portland cement. The name is recorded in a directory of 1823, and in his patent of 1824 Joseph Aspdin used it because the hardened cement resembled Portland stone, quarried on the Isle of Portland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>. His product was nothing like modern Portland cement and has been called proto-Portland cement.</p><p>His son William Aspdin, who left his father's company, apparently produced calcium silicates by accident in the 1840s, set up a plant at Rotherhithe in 1843 and improved his cement in 1848. In 1853 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Isaac Charles Johnson refined the product further and claimed to be its real father. In 1859 John Grant set requirements for the cement used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> sewer project, which became a specification, and Frederick Ransome patented the rotary kiln in 1885, allowing a continuous process.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Portland cement is made by heating limestone and a clay source in a kiln to form clinker, at about 1,450 °C for modern cements, and then grinding the clinker with about 5 per cent gypsum. The gypsum prevents flash setting. Ordinary Portland cement is grey, and white Portland cement needs raw materials very low in iron and a hotter kiln.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Its most common use is in concrete, a composite of gravel, sand, cement and water that can be cast in almost any shape and used for roads, dams and structural elements. It is also used in mortars, plasters and grouts. A typical concrete sets in about six hours and reaches a compressive strength of 8 MPa in 24 hours and 41 MPa at three months. Manufacture releases carbon dioxide from the raw materials and from fuel, and its quarries and kilns have environmental effects.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Portland cement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1824</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Joseph Aspdin · William Aspdin</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The name Portland cement was recorded in a directory of 1823 with a William Lockwood. Aspdin’s cement has been called a proto-Portland cement: his son William is regarded as the inventor of the modern form, and Isaac Charles Johnson claimed to be its real father.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/reinforced-concrete">Reinforced concrete<span>1853</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=portland-cement">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
